Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Find the index of the first adjective (word that ends with 'e' and is not the last word)
  let adjectiveIndex = -1;
  for (let I = 0; I < words.length - 1; i++) {
    const lastChar = words[i].slice(-1);
    if (lastChar === 'e' && !words[i + 1].endsWith('e')) {
      adjectiveIndex = i;
      break;
    }
  }

  // If no adjectives found, return original sentence
  if (adjectiveIndex === -1) return sentence;

  // Swap the positions of the first and second adjectives
  const firstAdjective = words.splice(adjectiveIndex, 2, ...words.slice(adjectiveIndex - 1, adjectiveIndex + 1).reverse()).join(" ");

  // Join the words back into a sentence
  return `${firstAdjective} ${sentence.slice(0, adjectiveIndex)} ${sentence.slice(adjectiveIndex + 2)}`;
}

// Example usage
const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const reorderedSentence = switchAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(reorderedSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

The `switchAdjectives()` function takes a string as input (the sentence), splits it into an array of words, finds the index of the first adjective, swaps its position with the next adjective (if any), reverses the order of the swapped words, and then joins the words back into a sentence with the original part before the adjectives and after them. The function returns the modified sentence.

This implementation handles cases where there are multiple adjectives in a row by finding the first one and swapping its position with the next one. It

