Here's a JavaScript function that takes a string variable in dot notation and returns all entries from an object or array where the nested key-value pair matches the provided string:

```javascript
function findEntries(obj, str) {
  let result = [];
  const keys = str.split('.');
  let currentObj = obj;

  for (let I = 0; I < keys.length - 1; i++) {
    if (!currentObj[keys[i]]) {
      return result; // Break out of the loop if we encounter a missing key
    }
    currentObj = currentObj[keys[i]];
  }

  const lastKey = keys[keys.length - 1];
  const values = Object.values(currentObj);
  const filteredValues = values.filter((val) => String(val).includes(str.slice(str.lastIndexOf('.') + 1)));

  for (let j = 0; j < filteredValues.length; j++) {
    const entry = {};
    for (let k = 0; k <= keys.length - 1; k++) {
      if (k === keys.length - 2) {
        entry[keys[k]] = filteredValues[j];
      } else {
        entry[keys[k]] = currentObj;
      }
    }
    result.push(entry);
  }

  return result;
}
```

The `findEntries()` function takes two arguments: the object/array to search through (`obj`) and the string to match against (`str`). The function first splits the string into an array of keys using the `split()` method. It then iterates over these keys and checks whether each key exists in the current object (`currentObj`). If any key is missing, the function immediately returns an empty `result` array. Otherwise, it keeps traversing the object until it reaches the final key. Once the final key is reached, it extracts the values associated with that key and filters them based on whether they contain the substring that was passed as the second argument to the function. Finally, it creates a new object for each matching value and adds it to the `result` array. Here's how you can use this function

