Here's the C code snippet to convert `argv` (which is an array of null-terminated strings passed as command-line arguments) into a single null-terminated string:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>

char *concatenate_args(int argc, char **argv) {
    size_t total_length = 0;
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        total_length += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add space between arguments and null terminator at the end
    }
    char *result = malloc(total_length);
    char *cur = result;
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        strcpy(cur, argv[i]);
        cur += strlen(argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) { // Don't add space before last argument
            *cur++ = ' ';
        }
    }
    *cur = '\0';
    return result;
}
```

This function takes `argc` (the number of command-line arguments) and `argv` (an array containing the arguments themselves) as parameters, concatenates all the arguments with spaces in between, and returns a new null-terminated string. The memory for this new string is allocated dynamically using `malloc()`. Make sure to free this memory when you no longer need it!

