Here's an implementation of the requested decorator using NestJS:

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';
import { ExecutionContext, NestMiddleware } from '@nestjs/core';

@Injectable()
export class DevModeMiddleware implements NestMiddleware {
  use(context: ExecutionContext, next: () => any) {
    const req = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();

    if (req.app.get('env') === 'development') {
      return next();
    } else {
      return false;
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
@AppController()
class AppController {
  constructor(private readonly app: AppService) {}

  @Get()
  @UseGuards(AuthGuard('jwt')) // Authentication guard
  @UseInterceptors(LoggerInterceptor) // Request logging interceptor
  @DevModeMiddleware() // Development mode middleware
  getHello(): string {
    return this.app.getHello();
  }
}
```

In this implementation, we create a new middleware called `DevModeMiddleware`. It takes the `ExecutionContext` and a callback function `next`, which is called to pass control to the next middleware or controller.

We retrieve the `Environement` from the `app` object passed in the `ExecutionContext` and compare it with the desired value ('development' in this case). If it matches, we call the `next` function to continue execution. Otherwise, we simply return `false` to prevent further execution.

This middleware can be used as any other middleware in your controllers by applying it as a decorator. In this example, we apply it after authentication and request logging middlewares. This allows us to easily disable certain features in production without modifying the codebase.

Note that you might want to modify the middleware to suit your specific needs, such as returning an error instead of `false` when running in production. Also, you may choose to extract the environment checking logic into a separate service for better maintainability.

