Here's an implementation of `RedisClient` class based on your requirements:

```javascript
const redis = require('redis');

class RedisClient {
  constructor() {
    this.client = new redis();
    this.client.on('error', (err) => {
      console.error(`Error: ${err}`);
    });
  }

  isAlive() {
    return this.client.ping().then(() => true).catch(() => false);
  }

  get(key) {
    return this.client.get(key);
  }

  set(key, value, ttl) {
    return this.client.setex(key, ttl, value);
  }

  del(key) {
    return this.client.del(key);
  }
}

const redisClient = new RedisClient();
module.exports = redisClient;
```

In this implementation, we're using the official `redis` package to connect to Redis and handle errors by listening to the `error` event. The `isAlive` method checks if the connection is alive by executing a `PING` command and returning a promise that resolves to `true` or rejects with an error. The other methods (`get`, `set`, and `del`) simply execute their respective commands and return promises as well. Note that the `set` method uses the `SETEX` command to set the value with an expiration time given by `ttl`. This is equivalent to calling `SET` followed by `EXPSET`.

